	Include file (in include/linux/bkpfs.h):
		Common header file used by both user and bkpfs kernel code
	Kernek code (fs/bkpfs):
		Folder clones from fs/wrapfs and augmented with necessary
		logic for handling backup of regular files

Note:
	BKPFS is loaded as a module i.e. (CONFIG_BKP_FS=m) in kernel.config
	file and an entry has been made in fs/Kconfig (line 19)
	and fs/Makefile (line 88).


Approach and design decisions:

Extended atrributes used in bkpfs:
	num_bytes: To track total number of bytes modified in file
		updated during vfs_write
	min_ver: To store the lowest(oldest) version number for the file
		updated during unlink(oldest)
	max_ver: To store the highest(newest) version number for file
		updated during create and unlink(newest/all)

1) When to backup?
	- The code for creating backups in included in bkpfs_write and
	bkpfs_close to allow for backup creation on file write and close.
	- During file write, if bytes modified (i.e. num_bytes) is more
	than threshold (1024) bytes, then a backup of the file is created.
	- I have picked 1024 to align with PAGE_SIZE(4096) and moreover to
	handle tradeoff between excessive backups and loss of data since 1024
	is a moderate size for write and user code usually handles write around
	that size in iterations.
	- The number of bytes is conditioned to return value from vfs_write
	for the original file and that value is used to update the
	xattr(num_bytes) since it will help to store the number of bytes
	modified in the file and create a backup accordingly.
	- However, if user writes less than 1024 bytes and closes the file,
	a backup would not be created. To avoid this and to ensure backup
	importance, I have added support for backup creation on file close
	if user has written positive bytes (i.e. num_bytes > 0).
	- If a backup file is created following changes occur:
		num_bytes is reset to 0 to indicate file upto this state
		has been backed up
		max_ver is incremented by 1 to indicate new backup file
	- If the backup file with same name already exists, it is overwritten.

2) Where to store backups?
	- Currently, the backups are stored in the same bkpfs path as the
	original file.
	- The backup files have been named as: .FILE_v$num
		where FILE is the original file name and $num is file's
		version num.
	- During creation num increases by 1, during deletion num decreases
	by 1.

3) Visibility policy
	- To ensure version file hiding from the normal user in ls and
	rm command, we add a callback(filldir) in readdir to filter
	version file names.
	- We check for following conditions to filter version filenames:
		1) File name starts with .(PREFIX)
		2) File name len atleast 4 (PREFIX+SUFFIX)
		3) Fil name ends with a number
		4) The end number is preceeded by _v(SUFFIX)
	- We return EINVAL for such version files that match the
	above conditions.

4) Retention policy:
	- If a new backup has to be created and the existing number of backups
	is more or equal to maxver for current mount, then we unlink the
	oldest file till num backups is one less than maxver to accomodate
	new backup file.
	- We use xattrs (min_ver and max_ver) to get the total number of backups
	for the file and then iteratively call unlink for oldest file till above
	condition is met.
	- If a backup file in unlinked following changes occur:
		min_ver is decremented by 1 to indicate old file unlink

	- We do not modify the file names, as it would be a cumbersome process
	and incur additional overhead. And I believe we have a steady versions
	handling mechanism so file renames can be avoided.

5) Vesion management:
	All the following functions have been supported using customized
	ioctl calls in user code and an ioctl for each function is defined
	in common header file.

	Following functions have been supported by the bkfs module:
	1) list versions
	- This function list all the version files that exist
	for the original file.
	- If no version exist an empty list is returned, otherwise an errno
	is returned

	2) Delete version
	- This function deletes the specified version for the file.
	- If oldest is specified - delted the min_ver and increments
	min_ver by 1.
	- If newest is specified - delted the min_ver and decrements max_ver by 1.
	- If all is specified - delted the max_ver iteratively and
	decrements max_ver by 1 in each iteration.

	3) View version
	- This function stores the data from the required version file in the
	user buffer in loop of size mentioned by user.
	- We pass a pos variable initialized with 0 from user code when the
	ioctl is called for the first time, then for each ioctl calls we update
	pos by number of bytes read from the file and return 0 if EOF is reached
	and set pos to 0 in that case.
	- The pos variable helps retain the seek position between multiple and
	successive ioctl calls.

	4) Restore version
	- This function restored the required version file in a new file
	which can be accessed, modified and copied by the user easily.
	- The restore file name is the same as the corresponding version
	file name with the prefix eliminated (.FILE_v$num -> FILE_v$num).

6) Mount options support:
	- To include support for max versions during mounting of bkpfs
	by the user, I have modified the bkpfs_mount function.
	- To add max versions, we add a new attribute in superblock info
	along with lower superblock, which stores the maxver supported
	in the current mount.
	- In bkpfs_mount the -o(option) string is validated and the number is
	extracted from the string and set in superblock info.
	- The verison provided must be between minver(=1) and maxver(=20)
	mentioned in fs/bkpfs/bkpfs.h file.

7) Dry run of bkpfs:
	- Run "make" in hw2-nidnani folder
	- Run "insmod /usr/src/hw2-nidnani/fs/bkpfs/"
	- Run "mount -t bkpfs -o maxver=5 /usr/src/hw2-nidnani/test_dir/ /mnt/bkpfs/"
	to mount bkpfs in /mnt/bkpfs/
	- To test bkpctl, you can run any of the create_*.c files by passing
	the mnt path for the file(new/existing) which then creates zero or more
	versions of it and then run bkpctl with the appropriate flags to check
	the functionalities provided.


Notes for running test scripts(IMPORTANT to reproducing correct results):
1) Run the Makefile in CSE-506 first to compile all the C helper code files

2a) For running scripts [1-35], change the macro BKPFS_NUM_BYTES_THRESHOLD in
fs/bkpfs/bkpfs.h (line 39) to 10, then run make in hw2-user and then run the
scripts to produce correct results and verify the functionality.

2b) For running scripts [36-38], revert the macro BKPFS_NUM_BYTES_THRESHOLD to
1024, then make in hw2-user, and then run the script to produce appropriate
results.

3) For script [2] - do insmod, mount, touch file in mnt dir as root, then login
to student and run scripts to get appropriate result.

4) Also certain variables have been included in ~/.bashrc file to assist with
the test scripts which include:
	export BKPFS_TEST_FILE='test_bkpfs'
	export BKPFS_MNT_VERSIONS=5
	export BKPFS_MNT_DIR='/mnt/bkpfs'
It is neccesary to have these veriables in bashrc file for the scripts to work

5) In case of confusion with the script results, refer to the corresponding
script, wherein I have mentioned the expected behavior in comments at the top.
		"Hope it is not an issue :)"

Testing(test_scripts/):
